			<div class="content" id="post-content-1566711">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Aside, using an author's past works to attack their opinion of an article you like is quite lame, don't you think?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's a greyish area. You can certainly form opinions on how &quot;worthwhile&quot; someone else's opinion is to you, based on your observations of their past works and comments, but actually dismissing them in comments along the lines of &quot;your articles suck, your comments suck, you suck&quot; counts as an <em>ad hominem</em> attack, which we don't allow.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-669416-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-669416-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<p>I don't think there was any <em>real</em> malice here, but don't do it again in the future, mkay?</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-669416-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-669416-1')">1</a>. As an aside, works should be judged on their own merit, not by who the author is or what the author has written before.</div>
</div>

